  "infoVideoLinksImportantBullet2": "Long distance = active converters / fiber often required.",
  "infoVideoLinksCompareTitle": "DP vs HDMI (very simplified)",
  "infoVideoLinksCompareBullet1": "HDMI: ubiquitous (TVs, processors), but EDID/HDCP can get in the way.",
  "infoVideoLinksCompareBullet2": "DisplayPort: very common on PCs, often very \"capable\" in terms of throughput.",
  "infoPcieGpuParagraph": "For video/mapping servers: GPU + bus + drivers = stability. Typical problems: drivers, cables, conversion, and output limitations.",
  "infoPcieGpuFieldTitle": "Land",
  "infoPcieGpuFieldBullet1": "Block a stable driver version before a big show.",
  "infoPcieGpuFieldBullet2": "Avoid \"cheap\" adapters.",
  "infoPcieGpuFieldBullet3": "Test at the show's actual resolution/FPS.",
  "infoChecklistQuickTitle": "Fast",
  "infoChecklistBullet1": "Correct cable/standard.",
  "infoChecklistBullet2": "Storage adapted to the flow.",
  "infoChecklistBullet3": "Stable drivers.",
  "infoChecklistBullet4": "Real test before show.",
  "infoChecklistBullet5": "Avoid cheap adapters.",
  "infoFooterNote": "Indicative info (field). Exact capacities vary according to equipment/versions.\nObjective: simple reference points + reliable method.",
  "ipBasicsPageTitle": "Network - IP bases / mask / DHCP (essential)",
  "ipBasicsCopyMemoTooltip": "Copy memo",
  "ipBasicsCopyChecklistTooltip": "Copy the checklist",
  "ipBasicsMemo1": "IP = logical address to reach a device on a network.",
  "ipBasicsMemo2": "Private (LAN): 10.x.x.x, 172.16-31.x.x, 192.168.x.x (not routed to the Internet).",
  "ipBasicsMemo3": "Mask / subnet = tells you which part is \"network\" vs. \"machine\".",
  "ipBasicsMemo4": "Same subnet = direct communication. Otherwise → gateway (router).",
  "ipBasicsMemo5": "DHCP assigns IP automatically; static = useful for nodes/console.",
  "ipBasicsToc1": "1) What is an IP and what is it used for?",
  "ipBasicsToc2": "2) Why 192.x / 10.x / 172.x? (private addresses)",
  "ipBasicsToc3": "3) Mask & subnetting: understanding for real",
  "ipBasicsToc4": "4) DHCP, gateway, DNS: who does what?",
  "ipBasicsToc5": "5) How two devices talk to each other (LAN vs. non-LAN)",
  "ipBasicsToc6": "6) Simple IP \"show\" plans (ready-to-copy examples)",
  "ipBasicsToc7": "7) Troubleshooting: symptoms → causes (field method)",
  "ipBasicsToc8": "8) Mini-exercises (quickly check a mask / a network)",
  "ipBasicsToc9": "9) Quick checklist",
  "ipBasicsSection1Paragraph": "An IP address (IPv4) is a logical identifier on a network. It is used to say \"send this packet to this device\".\n\nWhen you plug in a console, an Art-Net/sACN node, a PC, a managed switch... everything that talks on a network needs to have a consistent IP so that devices can find each other.",
  "ipBasicsSection1CalloutTitle": "Mental image",
  "ipBasicsSection1Bullet1": "MAC = hardware identifier (network card).",
  "ipBasicsSection1Bullet2": "IP = \"postal\" address on a network (changeable).",
  "ipBasicsSection1Bullet3": "Mask = \"district / zip code\" (defines subnetwork).",
  "ipBasicsSection1Bullet4": "Gateway = \"neighborhood exit\" (router).",
  "ipBasicsSection1Subtitle": "IPv4 in 10 seconds",
  "ipBasicsSection1SubBullet1": "Format: A.B.C.D (4 numbers from 0 to 255).",
  "ipBasicsSection1SubBullet2": "Ex: 192.168.0.50",
  "ipBasicsSection1SubBullet3": "It's not magic: it's 32 bits (4 bytes).",
  "ipBasicsSection1SubBullet4": "What counts for you: IP + mask must be consistent between devices.",
  "ipBasicsSection2Paragraph": "Some IPv4 address ranges are reserved for private networks (LANs). They are not routed over the Internet. That's why they're used in shows: you can set up your own local network without \"consuming\" public IPs.",
  "ipBasicsSection2CalloutTitle": "The 3 large private beaches (LAN)",
  "ipBasicsSection2Bullet1": "10.0.0.0 → 10.255.255.255 (often written \"10.x.x.x\")",
  "ipBasicsSection2Bullet2": "172.16.0.0 → 172.31.255.255 (often \"172.16-31.x.x\")",
  "ipBasicsSection2Bullet3": "192.168.0.0 → 192.168.255.255 (often \"192.168.x.x\")",
  "ipBasicsSection2Subtitle1": "Why do we often see 192.168.x.x?",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet1": "Consumer box/routers often use 192.168.0.x or 192.168.1.x.",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet2": "This has become a \"de facto standard\" in small LANs.",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet3": "But in show, 10.x.x.x is handy if you want lots of addresses or several VLANs.",
  "ipBasicsSection2Subtitle2": "And the famous 2.x.x.x (Art-Net)?",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet4": "Many Art-Net devices leave the factory with an IP 2.x.x.x (closed network).",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet5": "It's \"practical\" in plug&play (without DHCP), but surprising if your PC is in 192.168.x.x.",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet6": "Important: there's nothing magical about \"2.x\" for broadcast/unicast.",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet7": "What changes the scope of the broadcast is the mask (often /8 = 255.0.0.0 in legacy).",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet8": "Example: network 2.0.0.0/8 → broadcast = 2.255.255.255 (very large).",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet9": "Simple solution: put PC/console in the same range + same mask, or reconfigure the node on a 192/10 plane.",
  "ipBasicsSection3Paragraph": "The subnet mask tells you which addresses are \"in the same network\". Without a mask, an IP doesn't mean much.\n\nGround rule: if two devices are on the same subnet, they talk directly to each other. Otherwise, they need a gateway (router).",
  "ipBasicsSection3Callout1Title": "Most common masks",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet1": "255.0.0.0 ( /8 ) → 10.x.x.x typical \"large\" network",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet2": "255.255.0.0 ( /16 ) → 10.10.x.x or 192.168.x.x \"large\" network",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet3": "255.255.255.0 ( /24 ) → network 192.168.0.x \"classic\" (254 devices)",
  "ipBasicsSection3Callout2Title": "Mask → broadcast address (very useful to understand)",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet4": "Broadcast depends on the subnet, i.e. the mask (not \"2 vs 192\").",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet5": "192.168.10.0/24 → broadcast = 192.168.10.255 (small network).",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet6": "2.0.0.0/8 (255.0.0.0) → broadcast = 2.255.255.255 (huge network).",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet7": "The \"bigger\" the network, the more noise a broadcast can make (if you use it often).",
  "ipBasicsSection3Subtitle1": "Concrete example (most useful): /24",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet1": "IP: 192.168.0.50",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet2": "Mask: 255.255.255.0 ( /24 )",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet3": "Network: 192.168.0.0",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet4": "Devices \"in the same network\": 192.168.0.1 → 192.168.0.254",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet5": "If you switch to 192.168.1.50 → it's NOT the same network.",
  "ipBasicsSection3Subtitle2": "Why subnetwork?",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet6": "Organization: separate \"light\", \"video\", \"intercom\", \"internet\"...",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet7": "Performance: limit noise (broadcast/multicast) to one group.",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet8": "Security: prevent \"guest\" PCs from seeing/disturbing the show network.",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet9": "Diagnostics: easier to know \"where\" a device is.",
  "ipBasicsSection3Callout3Title": "Classic trap",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet8": "Same IP \"start\" ≠ same network if mask is not identical.",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet9": "Ex: 10.0.0.5/8 and 10.0.0.200/24 → not the same logical perimeter.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Subtitle1": "DHCP (automatic allocation)",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet1": "A DHCP server \"gives\": IP + mask + gateway + DNS.",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet2": "Practical: you plug it in, it works.",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet3": "Risk show: if DHCP drops/changes/conflicts → weird symptoms.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Subtitle2": "Static (fixed IP)",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet4": "You define IP + mask (and possibly gateway/DNS).",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet5": "Very useful for: console, nodes, managed switch, AP Wi-Fi show.",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet6": "Risk: duplicate IP if plan unclear (conflict).",
  "ipBasicsSection4Callout1Title": "Gateway",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet1": "This is the router's address: it's used to go \"outside the local network\".",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet2": "If you set up an isolated show network (without Internet) → gateway often useless.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet3": "But if you want to exit to another VLAN / Internet → essential.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Callout2Title": "DNS",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet4": "DNS = \"directory\": transforms a name (e.g. example.com) into an IP.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet5": "In show (Art-Net/sACN), often useless: you work in direct IP.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet6": "If you use services (upgrades, licenses, time server, streaming) → DNS becomes useful again.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Subtitle3": "Simple rule (beginner-friendly)",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet7": "Small closed show: static everywhere, no gateway, no DNS.",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet8": "Show with Internet / multiple networks: controlled DHCP + DHCP reservations, or static plan + router.",
  "ipBasicsSection5Paragraph": "When A wants to talk to B, it looks at the mask :\n- If B is in the same subnet → A sends directly to the local network.\n- If B is outside the subnet → A sends to the gateway (router), which takes care of the rest.",
  "ipBasicsSection5Callout1Title": "Example (easy)",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet1": "A = 192.168.0.10 /24",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet2": "B = 192.168.0.50 /24 → same network → OK direct.",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet3": "B = 192.168.1.50 /24 → different network → router/gateway needed.",
  "ipBasicsSection5Subtitle": "Why doesn't it ping?",
  "ipBasicsSection5SubBullet1": "Different mask → A thinks B is \"somewhere else\" (or vice versa).",
  "ipBasicsSection5SubBullet2": "No gateway → impossible to exit subnet.",
  "ipBasicsSection5SubBullet3": "PC firewall → ping blocked (Windows very frequent).",
  "ipBasicsSection5SubBullet4": "Cable / switch / VLAN: physically not in the same segment.",
  "ipBasicsSection5Callout2Title": "Broadcast vs. unicast (useful bonus)",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet4": "Unicast: to a specific (clean) IP.",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet5": "Broadcast: \"to all\" on the subnet (noisy).",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet6": "In show: broadcast may work in small, but quickly becomes a source of noise in large networks.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Subtitle1": "Plan A - ultra-simple (single network /24)",
  "ipBasicsSection6Code1": "Network: 192.168.10.0/24 (mask 255.255.255.0)\n\nConsole: 192.168.10.10\nPC (control): 192.168.10.20\nNode 1: 192.168.10.101\nNode 2: 192.168.10.102\nManaged switch: 192.168.10.2\nGateway: (empty) or 192.168.10.1 if router present",
  "ipBasicsSection6Callout1Title": "Why it works",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet1": "Everyone in the same subnet → no surprises.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet2": "Easy to explain, easy to troubleshoot.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet3": "Ideal for beginners / small to medium-sized shows.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Subtitle2": "Plan B - \"10.x\" when you want to grow up",
  "ipBasicsSection6Code2": "Network: 10.10.0.0/16 (mask 255.255.0.0)\n\nConsole: 10.10.0.10\nPC: 10.10.0.20\nNodes: 10.10.1.10 → 10.10.1.200\nSwitch: 10.10.0.2",
  "ipBasicsSection6Callout2Title": "When to use it",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet4": "When you have a lot of equipment (or several zones).",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet5": "When you plan VLAN later (light/video/IT).",
  "ipBasicsSection6Subtitle3": "Plan C - compat Art-Net \"2.x.x.x\" (legacy)",
  "ipBasicsSection6Code3": "Network: 2.0.0.0/8 (mask 255.0.0.0)\n\nPC/console: 2.0.0.10\nNode 1: 2.0.0.100\nNode 2: 2.0.0.101\n\n⚠️ Isolated network, no Internet, no routing.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Callout3Title": "Attention",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet6": "This plan is practical for older / default equipment.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet7": "But don't mix it up with a \"normal\" LAN (192.168.x.x) without a router.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Callout1Title": "Symptoms → probable causes",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet1": "\"I see the node but it's not responding\" → different mask / VLAN / firewall.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet2": "\"A device works then disappears\" → DHCP unstable / IP conflict.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet3": "\"Nothing works except on the switch\" → IP not in the same network / wrong cable / VLAN port.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet4": "\"Everything crawls / losses\" → broadcast/multicast too wide / low-end switch / network loop.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet5": "\"It works on wired but not on Wi-Fi\" → jitter / roaming / saturated band / power saving.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Subtitle": "5-minute pro method",
  "ipBasicsSection7SubBullet1": "1) Check IP + mask of console/PC and node.",
  "ipBasicsSection7SubBullet2": "2) Check: same subnet (e.g. 192.168.10.x /24)",
  "ipBasicsSection7SubBullet3": "3) Ping (if possible) + test access to node's web interface.",
  "ipBasicsSection7SubBullet4": "4) If DHCP: list who has which IP (DHCP table) and check for duplicates.",
  "ipBasicsSection7SubBullet5": "5) If sACN multicast: check IGMP snooping/querier (otherwise flood).",
  "ipBasicsSection7Callout2Title": "Very common trap",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet6": "Inherited mask on a PC (255.255.0.0 instead of 255.255.255.0).",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet7": "Result: you think you're \"in the same network\", but not like the other device.",
  "ipBasicsSection8Subtitle1": "Exercise 1: same network or not (/24)",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet1": "A = 192.168.10.50 /24",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet2": "B = 192.168.10.200 /24 → ✅ same network",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet3": "B = 192.168.11.200 /24 → ❌ not the same network",
  "ipBasicsSection8Subtitle2": "Exercise 2: /16 (255.255.0.0)",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet4": "A = 10.10.5.10 /16",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet5": "B = 10.10.200.20 /16 → ✅ same network (10.10.x.x)",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet6": "B = 10.11.200.20 /16 → ❌ not the same network",
  "ipBasicsSection8CalloutTitle": "Visual tip",
  "ipBasicsSection8Bullet1": "/24 → first 3 identical numbers (A.B.C) = same network.",
  "ipBasicsSection8Bullet2": "/16 → first 2 identical numbers (A.B) = same network.",
  "ipBasicsSection8Bullet3": "/8 → 1st identical number (A) = same network.",
  "ipBasicsSection8Subtitle3": "Exercise 3: avoiding \"weird\" IPs",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet7": "0.x.x.x: reserved (not a normal host IP).",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet8": "127.x.x.x: loopback (your PC itself).",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet9": "169.254.x.x: auto-IP when DHCP absent (APIPA) → sign of DHCP problem.",
  "ipBasicsSection9CalloutTitle": "Before looking for noon at 2 p.m.",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet1": "Same subnet = consistent IP + mask.",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet2": "No duplicate IPs (conflict).",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet3": "DHCP: OK if mastered; otherwise clear static plan.",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet4": "PC firewall: can block ping / discovery.",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet5": "Switch/VLAN: right port, right VLAN, no loop.",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet6": "sACN multicast: IGMP snooping/querier recommended.",
  "ipBasicsChecklistCopy": "Network - Quick checklist (IP)\n☐ All devices are in the same subnet (consistent IP + mask)\n☐ No duplicate IPs (conflict)\n☐ DHCP: either everyone in DHCP, or clear static plan (avoid fuzzy mix)\n☐ For Art-Net/sACN: dedicated network or VLAN if possible\n☐ Managed switch if multicast/IGMP (sACN)\n☐ Wi-Fi: avoid for critical data show (jitter), prefer wired\n☐ Simple test: ping + check mask/gateway",
  "ipBasicsFooterNote": "Objective: to understand enough to wire/configure/troubleshoot quickly on show.\nNetwork details can go deeper, but these basics cover 90% of field failures.",
  "aboutDmxPainterTerm120": "TERM\n120Ω",
  "aboutDmxPainterDaisyChainNote": "✅ Daisy-chain\n❌ No passive Y",
  "aboutDmxPainterXlr5Title": "XLR5 (DMX) - fast marker",
  "aboutDmxPainterPin1": "Pin 1: Shield / ground",
  "aboutDmxPainterPin2": "Pin 2: Data-",
  "aboutDmxPainterPin3": "Pin 3: Data++, Data+, Data+, Data+, Data+, Data+, Data+, Data+.",
  "aboutDmxPainterPin45": "Pin 4/5: \"data 2\" (rare / optional)",
  "aboutDmxPainterXlr3Practice": "In practice: XLR3 often used (1/2/3)",
  "aboutDmxPainterLastFixture": "LATEST\nAPPARATUS",
  "aboutDmxPainterTerminationLine": "Termination = 120Ω at end of line",
  "aboutDmxPainter120Between": "120Ω\nbetween\nData- and Data+",
  "aboutDmxPainterOneTermination": "✅ A single ending\n❌ Not in the middle\n❌ Not on each branch",
  "aboutDmxPainterConsoleIp": "CONSOLE\nArt-Net / sACN",
  "aboutDmxPainterSwitchSacn": "SWITCH\n(IGMP for sACN)",
  "aboutDmxPainterNode1": "NODE 1\nDMX OUT",
  "aboutDmxPainterNode2": "NODE 2\nDMX OUT",
  "aboutDmxPainterFix": "FIX",
  "aboutDmxPainterManyUniverses": "✅ Many universes\n✅ Nodes close to machines",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Paragraph": "Your objective: to ensure that each universe emitted by the source arrives at the right node (and DMX port) without ambiguity. Most Art-Net failures = bad universes, bad subnet/net, or broadcast saturation.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2CalloutTitle": "Simple method (avoids 80% of errors)",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Bullet1": "1) Write on paper: \"Universe 1 → Node A port 1\", \"Universe 2 → Node A port 2\", etc.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Bullet2": "2) Set stable IPs (DHCP reserved or static).",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Bullet3": "3) Use unicast if you have more than one node or a shared network.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Subtitle1": "Broadcast vs Unicast",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet1": "Broadcast: you send to everyone → easy but can \"flood\" a switch.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet2": "Unicast: you send to the node's IP → cleaner and more predictable.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet3": "En show: if in doubt → unicast.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Subtitle2": "Universe: hands-on",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet4": "Keep numbering simple (e.g. 1...N).",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet5": "Avoid \"one universe everywhere\": document your mappings.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet6": "On some setups, \"Net/Subnet/Universe\" exists: think \"mapping\", not \"magic\".",
  "aboutArtnetSection3CalloutTitle": "The \"real\" limits (in order)",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Bullet1": "Wi-Fi: variable latency + losses → flicker or stalls.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Bullet2": "Broadcast: overloads a LAN (everyone receives everything).",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Bullet3": "Low-end\" switch: limited buffers, mediocre multicast/broadcast management.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Bullet4": "Nodes: firmware/CPU limit (number of universes, frequency, options).",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Bullet5": "PC/source: driver, CPU load, antivirus, network card, etc.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Subtitle1": "Refresh speed",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet1": "Classic\" DMX is often ~20-44 fps depending on the frame.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet2": "On IP, you can send more, but the nodes don't always follow (and it's not always useful).",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet3": "Stability > frequency: stable at 30-40 fps is better than \"fast\" but unstable.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Subtitle2": "Simple rules",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet4": "Dedicated network (or VLAN).",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet5": "Unicast if several nodes.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet6": "Wired for critical shows.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Paragraph": "A node = IP → DMX. It can have 1, 2, 4, 8 DMX ports. Stability depends on the node AND the DMX wiring on the output side (termination, topology, splitters).",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Subtitle1": "Nodes vs Splitters",
  "aboutArtnetSection4SubBullet1": "Node: converts IP→DMX (and sometimes DMX→IP).",
  "aboutArtnetSection4SubBullet2": "DMX splitter: splits a DMX line into branches (opto recommended).",
  "aboutArtnetSection4SubBullet3": "Good design: IP as close as possible, then short, clean DMX.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4CalloutTitle": "RDM on Art-Net (variable support)",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Bullet1": "Some nodes support an RDM proxy: the console sees/configures RDM devices via IP.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Bullet2": "Others don't support RDM at all, or only partially.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Bullet3": "A \"classic\" opto splitter can block the RDM return (depending on the model).",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Bullet4": "Tip: if you want RDM, explicitly choose RDM-compatible hardware.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Subtitle2": "Good field reflexes",
  "aboutArtnetSection4SubBullet4": "If DMX unstable: DMX regulation first (termination, cable, passive Y), then IP only.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4SubBullet5": "If RDM unstable: disable RDM to check that pure DMX is stable.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5CalloutTitle": "Symptoms → probable causes",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Bullet1": "Flicker all over the set → Wi-Fi, broadcast flood, switch saturated.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Bullet2": "One node OK, the other not → universe/mapping, IP, unicast misconfigured.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Bullet3": "Random dropouts → faulty RJ45 cable, unstable switch, PC dropping packets.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Bullet4": "Single DMX port of a node KO → DMX cable, termination, device breaks line.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Bullet5": "RDM discovery impossible → node/splitter not RDM-compatible, DMX wiring \"borderline\".",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Subtitle": "Quick method",
  "aboutArtnetSection5SubBullet1": "1) Unicast to a single node (simple test).",
  "aboutArtnetSection5SubBullet2": "2) Check universe/mapping (paper + node config).",
  "aboutArtnetSection5SubBullet3": "3) Direct wired test (PC → switch → node) with known cable OK.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5SubBullet4": "4) Isolate DMX: node → 1 device + termination.",
  "aboutArtnetSection6Subtitle1": "Own architecture (IP → nodes → short DMX)",
  "aboutArtnetSection6Subtitle2": "Broadcast vs Unicast (idea)",
  "aboutArtnetAssetsCopyTitle": "Recommended assets (optional)",
  "aboutArtnetExampleLabel": "example",
  "aboutArtnetAssetsCopyTooltip": "Copy asset list",
  "aboutArtnetAssetsParagraph": "Section to illustrate the network (RJ45, switch, cables). If the assets don't exist, the app displays a clean fallback.",
  "aboutArtnetAssetsExamplesTitle": "Examples",
  "aboutArtnetAssetsRj45Label": "RJ45",
  "aboutArtnetAssetsRj45Hint": "Ethernet connector",
  "aboutArtnetAssetsSwitchLabel": "Switch",
  "aboutArtnetAssetsSwitchHint": "Switch (ideally managed if IGMP/VLAN)",
  "aboutArtnetAssetsCableLabel": "Ethernet cable",
  "aboutArtnetAssetsCableHint": "Cat5e/Cat6 = reliable base",
  "aboutArtnetChecklistCopyTitle": "Art-Net - Field checklist",
  "aboutArtnetChecklistCopyTooltip": "Copy the checklist",
  "aboutArtnetChecklistTitle": "Before you panic",
  "aboutArtnetChecklistBullet1": "Unicast if several nodes.",
  "aboutArtnetChecklistBullet2": "Dedicated network (or VLAN) if possible.",
  "aboutArtnetChecklistBullet3": "Wired for critical show.",
  "aboutArtnetChecklistBullet4": "Documented universe/mapping.",
  "aboutArtnetChecklistBullet5": "Simple IP test → 1 node → 1 device + termination.",
  "aboutArtnetChecklistBullet6": "If RDM: RDM proxy node confirmed + stable DMX wiring",
  "aboutArtnetFooterNote": "Indicative info (field). Depending on nodes/switch/firmware, behavior may vary.\nThe aim here is to understand and diagnose quickly.",
  "aboutArtnetDiagramSourceLabel": "SOURCE\n(PC/console)",
  "aboutArtnetDiagramSwitchLabel": "SWITCH",
  "aboutArtnetDiagramNode1Label": "NODE 1\nDMX OUT",
  "aboutArtnetDiagramNode2Label": "NODE 2\nDMX OUT",
