  "aboutDmxS2Title": "2) DMX frame - break, start code, channels",
  "aboutDmxS2PillBreak": "Break",
  "aboutDmxS2PillStartCode": "Start code",
  "aboutDmxS2PillSlots512": "512 slots",
  "aboutDmxS2PillRefresh": "Refresh",
  "aboutDmxS2Intro": "A DMX frame is sent in a loop. It begins with a \"break\" (silence), then a start code, then up to 512 values (0-255). Each fixture reads only the channels relevant to it (according to its address and mode).",
  "aboutDmxS2HowToReadTitle": "Read a frame (very simply)",
  "aboutDmxS2HowToReadB1": "Break + MAB: marks the start of the frame (resynchronization).",
  "aboutDmxS2HowToReadB2": "Start code: usually 0x00 (lighting data).",
  "aboutDmxS2HowToReadB3": "Slots 1→512: each slot = 1 channel (0-255).",
  "aboutDmxS2HowToReadB4": "The frame starts again continuously: it's normal to \"overwrite\" the old value.",
  "aboutDmxS2PracticalTitle": "In practice",
  "aboutDmxS2PracticalB1": "A 16-bit mode uses 2 channels for the same function (e.g. Pan coarse + Pan fine).",
  "aboutDmxS2PracticalB2": "The more universes you send, the greater the overall load (consoles/nodes/network).",
  "aboutDmxS2PracticalB3": "If you have flicker, think cable/termination/connections first before suspecting the console.",
  "aboutDmxS3Title": "3) DMX cabling - RS-485, topology, pinout",
  "aboutDmxS3PillRs485": "RS-485",
  "aboutDmxS3PillDaisyChain": "Daisy-chain",
  "aboutDmxS3PillNoY": "No Y",
  "aboutDmxS3Pill120ohm": "120 Ω",
  "aboutDmxS3Intro": "DMX512 is based on RS-485: a differential bus that likes clean lines. The golden rule: a daisy-chain from the source to the devices, terminated at the end.",
  "aboutDmxS3TopologyTitle": "Recommended topology",
  "aboutDmxS3TopologyB1": "Source → Device 1 → Device 2 → ... → last device.",
  "aboutDmxS3TopologyB2": "Avoid Y-branches: they create reflections (instability).",
  "aboutDmxS3TopologyB3": "If you need to make branches: use an opto-isolated DMX splitter.",
  "aboutDmxS3PinoutTitle": "Connectors (reminder)",
  "aboutDmxS3CablesTitle": "Cables: what really works",
  "aboutDmxS3CablesB1": "Use a DMX cable (impedance ~120 Ω), not a random microphone (except for short troubleshooting).",
  "aboutDmxS3CablesB2": "Keep lengths reasonable and avoid tight windings near power supplies/dimmers.",
  "aboutDmxS3CablesB3": "A bad cable = sometimes \"it works\"... until it doesn't (humidity, length, load).",
  "aboutDmxS4Title": "4) Termination & splitters - bus stability",
  "aboutDmxS4Intro": "On an RS-485 bus, termination prevents reflections at the end of the line. Opto-isolated splitters are used to create clean branches and protect the source.",
  "aboutDmxS4TerminatorTitle": "Termination (120 Ω): when and how",
  "aboutDmxS4TerminatorB1": "Terminate the last device (or XLR plug).",
  "aboutDmxS4TerminatorB2": "Only one termination per line (not everywhere).",
  "aboutDmxS4TerminatorB3": "Indispensable on long lines, heavy loads, noisy environments.",
  "aboutDmxS4SplittersTitle": "Splitters / opto-isolation",
  "aboutDmxS4SplittersB1": "Makes 2, 4, 8 branches without \"Y\".",
  "aboutDmxS4SplittersB2": "Electrically insulates: limits ground loops and protects the console/node.",
  "aboutDmxS4SplittersB3": "Handy for re-routing a signal over long distances.",
  "aboutDmxS5Title": "5) Troubleshooting - simple method (fast and reliable)",
  "aboutDmxS5Intro": "When a fixture doesn't respond, the key is to isolate: is it the address, the mode, the DMX line, or the fixture? Follow a short checklist before reconfiguring everything.",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickChecksTitle": "Quick checklist",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickB1": "Correct DMX address (and not 001 vs 000 according to the display).",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickB2": "Right DMX mode (number of channels)?",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickB3": "Good universe / output (console, node, port)?",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickB4": "Test with a known OK cable, then replace one by one.",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickB5": "Add/remove a termination at the end of the line to see the effect.",
  "aboutDmxS5IfFlickerTitle": "If it flickers / picks up",
  "aboutDmxS5IfFlickerB1": "Look for a hidden \"Y\", a mismatched splitter, or a bad XLR adapter.",
  "aboutDmxS5IfFlickerB2": "Keep DMX away from power supplies/dimmers, avoid large cable loops.",
  "aboutDmxS5IfFlickerB3": "Reduce complexity: 1 universe, 1 line, 1 device, then rebuild.",
  "aboutDmxS5GoldenRuleTitle": "Golden rule",
  "aboutDmxS5GoldenRuleBody": "You need to be able to operate a single device on a single line. If this basic test fails, the problem isn't \"the full patch\": it's the addressing, the wiring, or the hardware.",
  "aboutDmxS6Title": "6) Art-Net - DMX over IP (vocabulary and pitfalls)",
  "aboutDmxS6PillUdp": "UDP",
  "aboutDmxS6PillNodes": "Nodes",
  "aboutDmxS6PillBroadcast": "Broadcast",
  "aboutDmxS6PillUnicast": "Unicast",
  "aboutDmxS6Intro": "Art-Net transports DMX universes via Ethernet (UDP). A console/software sends universes over the network, and a node converts them into physical DMX outputs.",
  "aboutDmxS6BasicsTitle": "To remember",
  "aboutDmxS6BasicsB1": "One node = network ↔ DMX (several ports).",
  "aboutDmxS6BasicsB2": "Broadcast sends to everyone (simple but can saturate).",
  "aboutDmxS6BasicsB3": "Unicast sends only to the target node (often more stable on large rigs).",
  "aboutDmxS6BasicsB4": "Be careful when counting universes: Art-Net often starts at 0 (Universe 1 DMX = 0 Art-Net).",
  "aboutDmxS6WhenTitle": "When should you choose Art-Net?",
  "aboutDmxS6WhenB1": "Small/medium rigs, quick set-ups, lots of compatible hardware.",
  "aboutDmxS6WhenB2": "OK if the network is clean (correct switch, cables, no Wi-Fi).",
  "aboutDmxS6WhenB3": "If saturated: switch to unicast and segment the network (VLAN / dedicated network).",
  "aboutDmxS7Title": "7) sACN (E1.31) - multicast, priorities, own network",
  "aboutDmxS7PillE131": "E1.31",
  "aboutDmxS7PillMulticast": "Multicast",
  "aboutDmxS7PillPriority": "Priority",
  "aboutDmxS7PillIgmp": "IGMP",
  "aboutDmxS7Intro": "sACN (ANSI E1.31) is a modern standard for transporting DMX universes over IP. It is widely used in large systems, as it handles multicast and priorities well.",
  "aboutDmxS7KeyIdeasTitle": "Key ideas",
  "aboutDmxS7KeyIdeasB1": "Multicast: a universe is a network \"group\" (nodes subscribe to each other).",
  "aboutDmxS7KeyIdeasB2": "IGMP snooping on switches = indispensable on a large scale (otherwise it floods).",
  "aboutDmxS7KeyIdeasB3": "Priority: several sources possible, the one with the highest priority wins.",
  "aboutDmxS7KeyIdeasB4": "Like Art-Net: clean network + quality cables/switches = stability.",
  "aboutDmxS8Title": "8) DMX vs Art-Net vs sACN - summary (what to choose?)",
  "aboutDmxS8Intro": "Ultimately, all three do the same thing: control channels. The difference lies in distribution (DMX cable vs. network), scale and robustness.",
  "aboutDmxS8QuickTableTitle": "4-line summary",
  "aboutDmxS8QuickB1": "DMX: simple, robust, 1 cable = 1 line (512 channels).",
  "aboutDmxS8QuickB2": "Art-Net: very common, easy to deploy, beware of broadcasting.",
  "aboutDmxS8QuickB3": "sACN: more \"pro large systems\", multicast + priorities, requires a well-configured network.",
  "aboutDmxS8QuickB4": "In all cases: documentation + progressive testing = huge time savings.",
  "aboutDmxS8ChooseTitle": "Quick selection",
  "aboutDmxS8ChooseB1": "Small set-up: DMX direct or Art-Net (simple).",
  "aboutDmxS8ChooseB2": "Many universes: dedicated network + nodes; Art-Net in unicast or sACN.",
  "aboutDmxS8ChooseB3": "Very large rigs / infrastructure: sACN + switches/IGMP well managed.",
  "aboutSunTimesTitle": "Sunrise/sunset",
  "aboutSunTimesSubtitle": "Choice of city and date, sunrise/sunset times + thresholds (lux).",
  "homeBpmTitle": "BPM",
  "aboutReseauPageTitle": "The network - field benchmarks",
  "aboutReseauSummaryCopy": "NETWORK - field markers\n- RJ45: Cat5e/6/6A → 1G/10G depending on distance.\n- Fiber: long distance + EMI immunity.\n- Switch: IGMP important for multicast sACN.\n- VLAN: separate light/video/IT = stability.",
  "aboutReseauChecklistCopy": "NETWORK - Checklist\n☐ Clear IP plan (range / masks)\n☐ Correct switch (IGMP if multicast sACN)\n☐ VLAN if multiple \"worlds\" (light/video/IT)\n☐ Cables tested + tagged\n☐ Fiber: compatible modules + clean connectors\n☐ Avoid loops (STP) + avoid public Wi-Fi on show LAN",
  "aboutReseauChecklistCopyTooltip": "Copy",
  "aboutReseauFooterNote": "Indicative info (field). Exact capacities vary according to equipment / cables / modules.\nObjective: simple reference points + reliable method.",
  "aboutReseauToc1": "1) Network basics (LAN / IP / throughput)",
  "aboutReseauToc2": "2) RJ45 & categories (Cat5e/6/6A/...)",
  "aboutReseauToc3": "3) PoE (network power supply)",
  "aboutReseauToc4": "4) Fiber (SM/MM) + connectors (LC/SC)",
  "aboutReseauToc5": "5) SFP / SFP+ / QSFP (modules)",
  "aboutReseauToc6": "6) Switches (VLAN / IGMP / QoS)",
  "aboutReseauToc7": "7) Art-Net / sACN on network (tips)",
  "aboutReseauToc8": "8) Checklist",
  "aboutReseauSection1Title": "The 3 simple ideas",
  "aboutReseauSection1Bullet1": "IP = address (e.g. 10.0.0.50).",
  "aboutReseauSection1Bullet2": "LAN = local network via switch (not \"internet\").",
  "aboutReseauSection1Bullet3": "Throughput = capacity (1G, 10G...), but stability also depends on the switch.",
  "aboutReseauSection1Title2": "Topology show (clean)",
  "aboutReseauSection1Bullet4": "Console/PC → central switch → nodes/servers.",
  "aboutReseauSection1Bullet5": "Avoid random daisy-chain networks.",
  "aboutReseauSection1Bullet6": "If possible: a dedicated show network (not public Wi-Fi).",
  "aboutReseauSection2Title": "Quick reference points",
  "aboutReseauSection2Bullet1": "Cat5e: 1 Gbit/s \"classic\" (up to 100m).",
  "aboutReseauSection2Bullet2": "Cat6: 1G/10G (10G for shorter distances).",
  "aboutReseauSection2Bullet3": "Cat6A: 10G up to 100m (practical benchmark).",
  "aboutReseauSection2Bullet4": "Beyond that: Cat7/8 exist, but Cat6A is often the sweet spot in the field.",
  "aboutReseauSection2Title2": "Land",
  "aboutReseauSection2Bullet5": "Always test/label cables (especially when renting).",
  "aboutReseauSection2Bullet6": "Avoid damaged connectors: a \"tired\" RJ45 = phantom failures.",
  "aboutReseauSection2Bullet7": "Plan on 10G if you're doing heavy IP video.",
  "aboutReseauSection3Paragraph": "PoE can be used to power equipment via RJ45 (IP cams, Wi-Fi APs, small nodes, etc.). The critical point: the switch's total PoE budget.",
  "aboutReseauSection3Title": "Reference",
  "aboutReseauSection3Bullet1": "PoE (802.3af) ≈ 15W, PoE+ (802.3at) ≈ 30W, PoE++/bt higher.",
  "aboutReseauSection3Bullet2": "The switch has a total \"budget\" (e.g. 120W).",
  "aboutReseauSection3Bullet3": "If you go over budget: some ports no longer light up / reboot.",
  "aboutReseauSection4Title": "Why fiber in the show",
  "aboutReseauSection4Bullet1": "Very long distances.",
  "aboutReseauSection4Bullet2": "Immunity to interference (EMI), top in busy environments.",
  "aboutReseauSection4Bullet3": "Practical for connecting FOH ↔ set ↔ control room.",
  "aboutReseauSection4Title2": "Multimode vs. singlemode (single marker)",
  "aboutReseauSection4Bullet4": "Multimode (MM): \"medium\" distances (often building/platform).",
  "aboutReseauSection4Bullet5": "Single mode (SM): very long distances.",
  "aboutReseauSection4Bullet6": "SFP modules must match the fiber type.",
  "aboutReseauSection4Title3": "Connectors",
  "aboutReseauSection4Bullet7": "LC: small, very common on SFP.",
  "aboutReseauSection4Bullet8": "SC: larger, common in infrastructure.",
  "aboutReseauSection4Bullet9": "Always protect/clean (dust = losses).",
  "aboutReseauSection5Title": "Reference",
  "aboutReseauSection5Bullet1": "SFP: often 1G.",
  "aboutReseauSection5Bullet2": "SFP+: often 10G.",
  "aboutReseauSection5Bullet3": "QSFP/QSFP+: 40G (depending on use).",
  "aboutReseauSection5Bullet4": "A module = speed + fiber type + length (everything must be consistent).",
  "aboutReseauSection5Paragraph": "In practice: use switch-compatible modules (ideally from the same supplier/model) to avoid surprises.",
  "aboutReseauSection6Title": "Useful show functions",
  "aboutReseauSection6Bullet1": "VLAN: separate light / video / IT.",
  "aboutReseauSection6Bullet2": "IGMP snooping: indispensable if you use sACN multicast on a large scale.",
  "aboutReseauSection6Bullet3": "QoS: useful if you mix a lot of flows (depending on context).",
  "aboutReseauSection6Title2": "Common errors",
  "aboutReseauSection6Bullet4": "Cheap switch that floods multicast → network collapses.",
  "aboutReseauSection6Bullet5": "Network loop without STP → broadcast storm.",
  "aboutReseauSection6Bullet6": "Public Wi-Fi on the same LAN as the show → instability.",
  "aboutReseauSection7Title": "Art-Net (field)",
  "aboutReseauSection7Bullet1": "Often easy to set up.",
  "aboutReseauSection7Bullet2": "Broadcast possible on a small, dedicated LAN, but unicast is cleaner.",
  "aboutReseauSection7Bullet3": "Mapping universes/ports = point number 1 to check.",
  "aboutReseauSection7Title2": "sACN (land)",
  "aboutReseauSection7Bullet4": "Multicast: efficient, but requires a proper switch (IGMP).",
  "aboutReseauSection7Bullet5": "Priorities: useful if several sources.",
  "aboutReseauSection7Bullet6": "On large shows: sACN + IGMP is often the \"clean\" choice.",
  "aboutReseauChecklistTitle": "Fast",
  "aboutReseauChecklistBullet1": "Correct switch + clean cables.",
  "aboutReseauChecklistBullet2": "IGMP if sACN multicast.",
  "aboutReseauChecklistBullet3": "Separate usage (VLAN).",
  "aboutSacnPageTitle": "sACN / E1.31 - multicast & priorities",
  "aboutSacnCopyTooltip": "Copy markers",
  "aboutSacnChecklistCopyTooltip": "Copy",
  "aboutSacnFooterNote": "Indicative info (field). Behavior depends on switches (IGMP), nodes and firmware.\nObjective: understand and stabilize an sACN network.",
  "aboutSacnCopy1": "sACN (E1.31) = DMX over IP (UDP), show standard.",
  "aboutSacnCopy2": "Universe sACN = 1..63999 (standard range).",
  "aboutSacnCopy3": "Multicast: IGMP snooping recommended, otherwise flood.",
  "aboutSacnCopy4": "Priority: useful with multiple sources (console + backup).",
  "aboutSacnChecklistCopy": "sACN (E1.31) - Field checklist\n☐ Switch correct (ideally manageable)\n☐ IGMP snooping enabled if multicast\n☐ (If possible) IGMP querier present in VLAN (otherwise odd behavior possible)\n☐ Unicast if network not mastered / troubleshooting\n☐ Priorities: check multiple sources (console/backup)\n☐ Mapping universe ↔ ports node verified\n☐ Wi-Fi avoided in prod",
  "aboutSacnToc1": "1) What does sACN do?",
  "aboutSacnToc2": "2) Universe (numbering)",
  "aboutSacnToc3": "3) Multicast / Unicast + IGMP",
  "aboutSacnToc4": "4) Priorities (multi-sources)",
  "aboutSacnToc5": "5) Limits / performance",
  "aboutSacnToc6": "6) RDM & sACN (proxy / depending on equipment)",
  "aboutSacnToc7": "7) Schematics",
  "aboutSacnToc8": "8) Checklist",
  "aboutSacnSection1Pill1": "UDP",
  "aboutSacnSection1Pill2": "Multicast",
  "aboutSacnSection1Pill3": "Priority",
  "aboutSacnSection1Paragraph": "sACN (E1.31) is a widely used standard for transporting DMX universes over IP. It's often the preferred choice for clean network architecture: well-managed multicast, standardized priorities, and more \"pro network\" behavior.",
  "aboutSacnSection1Title": "When it's top",
  "aboutSacnSection1Bullet1": "Lots of universes on a well-managed network.",
  "aboutSacnSection1Bullet2": "Installations / large networks with several sources (priorities).",
  "aboutSacnSection1Bullet3": "When you want to avoid \"broadcast everywhere\".",
  "aboutSacnSection2Pill1": "Universe 1..63999",
  "aboutSacnSection2Pill2": "512 slots/universe",
  "aboutSacnSection2Paragraph": "One sACN universe corresponds to one DMX universe: up to 512 slots. The standard sACN numbering is 1..63999. In practice, the important thing is console ↔ node ↔ DMX port mapping.",
  "aboutSacnSection2Title": "Classic mistake",
  "aboutSacnSection2Bullet1": "You change the universe number on the console side, but not on the node side (or vice versa).",
  "aboutSacnSection2Bullet2": "You patch \"Universe 0\" when the hardware expects a base 1.",
  "aboutSacnSection2Bullet3": "You're mixing up numbering conventions between tools.",
  "aboutSacnSection3Paragraph": "sACN often uses multicast: each universe \"lives\" on a multicast group, and nodes subscribe to the universes they need.",
  "aboutSacnSection3Title": "IGMP snooping (why it's important)",
  "aboutSacnSection3Bullet1": "Without IGMP: the switch \"floods\" multicast everywhere → possible overload.",
  "aboutSacnSection3Bullet2": "With IGMP: switch sends only to subscribed ports → stable network.",
  "aboutSacnSection3Bullet3": "On large volumes: IGMP is often THE difference between \"it works\" and \"it's hell\".",
  "aboutSacnSection3Subtitle": "Diagram: multicast & IGMP",
  "aboutSacnSection3Subtitle2": "Unicast (option)",
  "aboutSacnSection3SubBullet1": "Possible depending on consoles/nodes.",
  "aboutSacnSection3SubBullet2": "Useful for troubleshooting or if multicast network is out of control.",
  "aboutSacnSection3SubBullet3": "Less elegant but sometimes simpler.",
  "aboutSacnSection4Pill1": "Source A prio 100",
  "aboutSacnSection4Pill2": "Source B prio 90",
  "aboutSacnSection4Paragraph": "sACN manages a notion of priority: if several sources send the same universe, the receiver keeps the highest-priority source. Very useful with a main console + a backup console + a media server.",
  "aboutSacnSection4Title": "Traps",
  "aboutSacnSection4Bullet1": "Two unwanted sources on the same universe → \"it fights\".",
  "aboutSacnSection4Bullet2": "Priority set incorrectly → backup takes over.",
  "aboutSacnSection4Bullet3": "Debug: cut a source and check who wins.",
  "aboutSacnSection5Pill1": "IGMP",
  "aboutSacnSection5Pill2": "Switch",
  "aboutSacnSection5Pill3": "Wi-Fi no",
  "aboutSacnSection5Pill4": "CPU node",
  "aboutSacnSection5Title": "What breaks first",
  "aboutSacnSection5Bullet1": "Multicast without IGMP → flood.",
  "aboutSacnSection5Bullet2": "Switch not adapted (buffers/CPU) → losses.",
  "aboutSacnSection5Bullet3": "Wi-Fi → jitter/losses.",
  "aboutSacnSection5Bullet4": "Limited nodes (ports, firmware).",
  "aboutSacnSection5Subtitle": "Simple rules",
  "aboutSacnSection5SubBullet1": "Managed switch + IGMP snooping if many universes.",
  "aboutSacnSection5SubBullet2": "Dedicated VLAN light if possible.",
  "aboutSacnSection5SubBullet3": "Unicast for troubleshooting.",
  "aboutSacnSection6Paragraph": "As with Art-Net, RDM \"over IP\" often depends on a proxy mechanism in the nodes. The sACN standard does not automatically imply that your node is doing RDM correctly.",
  "aboutSacnSection6Title": "Check the node documentation",
  "aboutSacnSection6Bullet1": "RDM proxy / RDM over IP support.",
  "aboutSacnSection6Bullet2": "Limitations (discovery only, partial commands).",
  "aboutSacnSection6Bullet3": "Compatible with splitters/opto on DMX side.",
  "aboutSacnSection6Subtitle": "Field method",
  "aboutSacnSection6SubBullet1": "Stabilize DMX without RDM.",
  "aboutSacnSection6SubBullet2": "Test RDM on a simple, clean line.",
  "aboutSacnSection6SubBullet3": "If problems arise: deactivate RDM, then isolate node/splitter.",
  "aboutSacnSection7Subtitle1": "Multicast + IGMP (idea)",
  "aboutSacnSection7Subtitle2": "Priorities (2 sources)",
  "aboutSacnChecklistTitle": "Before blaming \"protocol",
  "aboutSacnChecklistBullet1": "IGMP snooping enabled if multicast.",
  "aboutSacnChecklistBullet2": "Dedicated VLAN if possible.",
  "aboutSacnChecklistBullet3": "Unicast for troubleshooting.",
  "aboutSacnChecklistBullet4": "Consistent priorities if multiple sources.",
  "aboutSacnChecklistBullet5": "Wi-Fi avoided in prod.",
